SUMMARY:WNC Sierra Club 8/5: Floodplains and Riverbanks Post-Helene
DESCRIPTION:WNC Sierra Club\nPlease join us Aug. 5 as we host RiverLink's 
 leadership for a lively conversation about our region's flood resilience a
 nd streambank restoration efforts as we approach the two-year anniversary 
 of Hurricane Helene.\nRenee Fortner and Lisa Raleigh will discuss some of 
 the opportunities and challenges we face on our journey toward environment
 al and economic recovery\, including examples of what we're doing well and
  where we're falling short amid our region's ever-changing\, dynamic clima
 te and landscape.\n\nThe program will be held both in person at the Reuter
  Center at UNC Asheville and [online on Zoom](https://unca-edu.zoom.us/j/8
 5189349413?pwd=QmorsXPwjbS0BgiLR7xLItF3M1atab.1).\n\n**Renee Fortner** is 
 the Director of Programs at local environmental non-profit [RiverLink](htt
 ps://riverlink.org/). As an avid river paddler\, gardener\, and naturalist
 \, Renee is continually reminded how the environment around us and our dai
 ly lives are affected by access to clean\, abundant water. Through her rol
 e at RiverLink\, she builds collaborative partnerships to address the impa
 cts of climate change and human activities on our waterways and adjacent c
 ommunities. Recent collaborative projects include converting an underused 
 asphalt parking lot into a thriving wetland ecosystem on A-B Tech's campus
  and completion of the post-Helene Middle Swannanoa River restoration plan
 .\n\n**Lisa Raleigh\, PhD** is the Executive Director of RiverLink\, a con
 servation organization celebrating 40 years of dedication to the environme
 ntal and economic vitality of the French Broad River watershed. A hydrolog
 ist by training and passionate nonprofit leader by calling\, Lisa brings a
  lifetime shaped by the West — raised in Montana and spending thirty yea
 rs in Colorado's high country. For the past five years\, she has been hono
 red to carry forward RiverLink's mission alongside the extraordinary dedic
 ation of its staff\, Board and supporters — serving the French Broad Riv
 er watershed and the communities along its banks\, particularly during our
  region's ongoing post-Helene recovery.
